          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 全家",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "全家 — quánjiā — \"the whole family\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "今天我们全家一起打扫房子 jīntiān wǒmen quánjiā yìqǐ dǎsǎo fángzi — Today the whole family is cleaning the house together."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 全 (quán) — six strokes: a wide roof like 人, then a bar, a second bar, a vertical down from the first through the second, and a long bar at the foot. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "全家 (quánjiā) is built from 全 (quán) 'whole' and 家 'a home', both inherited from Middle Chinese, dzjwen and kae (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'complete'."
            }
          ]
        },
